hi there steve kaufman here  um today i want to talk about something  that i've been thinking about a lot uh  you know in the last little while and  that is the importance of content  and i'm going to use  anime in japanese as an example of how  content  and having a content strategy  is  fundamental to our language learning  success  now people learn languages for different  reasons a lot of people who learn  japanese are interested in anime and i'm  going to show you snippets of a video  that  one of the members of the uh the link  staff here who's interested in japanese  he put this together  and you can know then go  you can find the link in the description  box to go and see the whole video as to  how he prepares uh content from anime to  help with his japanese language learning  however  something like anime or we've recently  had a number of people  uh show us how to get  transcripts from movies how to provide  transcripts for videos  to enhance content  make it more lively make more appealing  to people  and so those are other examples of how  we use  content for language learning but but we  need a comprehensive strategy for  content because at different stages in  our learning  the content that we can access and the  way we use content is going to change  but having a conscious  content strategy is so important so i'm  going to give you some additional  examples  for example the person learning the  japanese after all the work he puts into  getting this anime  finds that the anime transcripts are  still too difficult for him so the anime  content there serves the purpose of of  sort of an incentive a goal  something that he wants to get to that  motivates him to learn japanese and so  when he finally is able to understand  more of it then it'll be very powerful  learning material for now it's just  something that he occasionally goes and  has a look at  it it reminds him of where he wants to  get to and then he realizes that he's  not good enough yet he doesn't have  enough words to cope with that as  learning content and beats a retreat  back into easier content  the same may be the case with  transcripts for movies or videos so it's  kind of out there  you know shining and sparkling something  we want to get to but not necessarily  yet something that we could use to learn  so let's take the other end of the  spectrum and i'm going to show you  an example  from my farsi  where i'm at such an early level in  farsi that i can't even have a  conversation  normal conversation however  i can by listening to these many stories  i can have a conversation built around  this specific bit of content  and so i read some of it which is  difficult to do because we don't have  text to speech for farsi at link  and then we discuss it so i'm able to  use this content again  to help me with my farsi  uh this is something that classroom  teachers could do they could ask their  students to you know study one or two  stories and if the students have the  patience to listen  as i do 30 40 50 times then they will  have an easier time reading this  material and talking about it in the  classroom  uh with my arabic where i'm a little  further along  i had a conversation unfortunately the  skype connection wasn't very good  and we were trying to talk because this  person my tutor is a syrian refugee who  lives in turkey and he was explaining a  little bit about the syrian refugees in  turkey and he runs  entrepreneurship seminars for these  refugees in turkey but there were a lot  of words that came up that i simply  don't know and if he gives me the words  during our lesson no matter how many  times he tells me the word or writes it  in the chat box and skype i can't  remember it i whereas so then i went  back to him i said you know could you  possibly create say 10  monologues or conversations or even 23  minutes long each about the plight of  syrian refugees in turkey the problems  they have and so forth and so on their  memories of home or whatever it might be  because typically  that kind of  conversation will use higher frequency  words so again it's a form of content  that's a little more advanced than my  many stories and if i were able to  listen to these  monologues conversations  many many times and read them then we  could have a conversation on that  subject so again i could use  content  as a  you know part of my strategy  to get me into speaking more  graded readers are a form of again  content strategy so i think the learner  should develop a content strategy based  on what their interests are based on  their level in the language but i think  also teachers online tutors or classroom  teachers should have a strategy on  because  on using content as as sort of the key  engine of instruction  because for example if i learn some  rules or try to learn some rules of  farsi grammar  and some words  and then try to have a conversation i  will fail  but if i have listened to the content  and read the content and i know the  meaning of the story  then i just simply use these phrases  and sentences  particularly the phrases and the words  in my answer without worrying about the  sort of grammar that underpins  what i am using  and at some point i can have a look for  reference to what these grammar  explanations are  but fundamentally it's it's sort of this  dynamic use of content that is enabling  me to get into the language to  understand more of the language and  eventually to start speaking  and even the speaking that i do  is  not so much to demonstrate  that i can speak but rather to sharpen  me so that the next time i listen to  this content and i try to remember the  words and the phrases i will be sharper  so this is just sort of i'm kind of at  the beginnings of this idea of content  how we you know our content strategy is  fundamental to our language learning  and and possibly again i throw this out  there  if we can collaborate on generating  content you know if for example i have  my arabic tutor produce 10 or 20 stories  of or conversations or monologues  i may have to pay him for this i mean i  pay him for my online discussions with  him then i would certainly put these in  our library at link and make them  available to other people as i have done  with the arabic and farsi and and  romanian and greek and ukrainian and  other content that i have  created for link but the more we  collaborate in providing content of  on a variety of subjects of different  types at different levels that we can  all share in it'll all help us develop  the content strategy that we need for  ourselves so i invite you to go and have  a look at what one of our people did  with regard to using anime as part of  his  content strategy  okay thank you for listening bye for now  i'm just going to say that rana is my  farsi tutor  my farsi is very limited so what we like  to do is that i read one of the mini  stories and then rana asks me questions  so i'm going to start doing that okay  right  great  dance darcy  there's a hot tom half tom half tom  um  okay  and  um  um  [Music]  uh  [Music]  questions  oh  um  um  um  um  [Applause]  [Music]  we'll get back to our lesson i'll just  stop the video just to show what we're  trying to do here to get my  farsi going  [Music]  you
 

